---
# Static interface metadata for Akvorado. REQUIRED — copy to exporters.yaml:
#
#   cp akvorado/exporters.yaml.example akvorado/exporters.yaml
#
# and edit before `docker compose --profile netflow up -d`. The orchestrator
# fails to start without it, which is intentional: NetFlow records carry an
# ifIndex and nothing else, and Akvorado DISCARDS every flow whose ifIndex it
# cannot resolve. A wrong value here looks exactly like "NetFlow isn't working"
# — an empty `flows` table with no error anywhere obvious — so it is better to
# refuse to start than to ship a plausible default.
#
# This file is a list, spliced into `outlet.metadata.providers` by the
# !include in akvorado.yaml. Using the `static` provider means no snmpd has to
# run on the router; this is what Akvorado's own docs recommend for software
# exporters such as softflowd.
#
# ── Finding the values ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `name:` must match the router's Prometheus `router` label (ROUTER_NAME or the
# name in ROUTER_TARGETS in .env), because the dashboard's Router variable is
# populated from Prometheus and then filtered against ExporterName here.
#
# Exporter address: the router's IP as seen by the monitoring host — the same
# address as ROUTER_IP in .env. softflowd sources its export packets from the
# interface it egresses on, so if that is not the router's LAN address, check
# the akvorado_inlet_flows_received_total metric's `exporter` label for the
# address actually arriving.
#
# ifIndex: do NOT guess. Once the router-side `netflow` profile is installed,
# openwrt-monitor-netflow-health.sh exports it to Prometheus:
#
#   openwrt_netflow_ifindex{router="...", interface="wan"}
#
# Or read it directly on the router:
#
#   cat /sys/class/net/br-lan/ifindex
#
# It is stable across reboots in practice but is NOT guaranteed to be; if flows
# stop resolving after a firmware upgrade, re-check it here first.
#
# ── Which interface, and why it is the LAN bridge ───────────────────────────
#
# Capture happens on br-lan, not on the WAN device. softflowd captures with
# libpcap at the device, and on WAN that is *after* SNAT -- every outbound flow
# would carry the router's public address as its source, and "which client is
# using the bandwidth" would be unanswerable. The bridge sees pre-NAT addresses.
#
# Do not list both br-lan and the WAN device: each packet crosses both, so every
# byte would be counted twice.
#
# ── Interface descriptions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The `description` feeds Akvorado's interface-classifiers, which set
# InIfBoundary. NOTE that softflowd sets if_index_in and if_index_out to the
# same value, so boundary is constant across every flow and cannot separate
# inbound from outbound. The Grafana dashboard therefore ignores boundary and
# derives direction from SrcNetRole/DstNetRole instead (see
# `clickhouse.networks` in akvorado.yaml).
#
# So the description below is cosmetic for our dashboard. It only affects
# Akvorado's own console, whose homepage happens to default to filtering
# InIfBoundary = 'external'; adjust the filter in the console UI rather than
# mislabelling the bridge as transit.

- type: static
  exporters:
    # Replace with your router's address. A /32 (or /128) pins it to one host;
    # a wider prefix would apply to every router in it.
    192.168.0.1/32:
      name: openwrt-main
      ifindexes:
        # <ifindex>: { name, description, speed }
        # speed is in Mbps and is only used to render link-utilisation
        # percentages; an approximate value is fine.
        #
        # This must be the ifIndex of the LAN BRIDGE, matching whatever
        # NETFLOW_INTERFACES resolved to on the router (br-lan by default). Read
        # the real number from the openwrt_netflow_ifindex metric, or on the
        # router with:  cat /sys/class/net/br-lan/ifindex
        3:
          name: br-lan
          description: "LAN bridge"
          speed: 1000

    # A second router (e.g. a downstream AP also running the netflow profile)
    # would be another entry here.
    # 192.168.0.2/32:
    #   name: openwrt-new
    #   ifindexes:
    #     3:
    #       name: br-lan
    #       description: "LAN bridge"
    #       speed: 1000
